    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7fe08f34-326d-53ce-9659-6093ab04f1fa",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$?\n\nI. $-\\sqrt{3}$\n\nII. $-0.2$\n\nIII. $0$\n\nIV. $-\\sqrt{4}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$ is the set of all negative rational numbers.\n\n- $-\\sqrt{3}$: It is negative, and it is an irrational real number. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$.\n\n- $-0.2=-\\frac{1}{5}$: It is negative, and it is a rational number but not an integer, since it can be written as $-\\frac{1}{5}$. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$.\n\n- $0$: Zero is not negative or positive, and this set does not include zero. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$.\n\n- $-\\sqrt{4}=-2$: It is negative, and it is an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is: II and IV only.",
